# crss host-local overlay config — example
#
# Copy this directory to $CRSS_HOME (default: $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/crss, or
# ~/.config/crss if XDG_CONFIG_HOME is unset) and edit config.sh for your
# host. See ../../examples/crss-overlay/README.md for the full picture
# (this file, local.md, the ~/.claude/rules/crss-host.md pointer, and the
# optional leak-denylist.txt for tests/test-no-host-leaks.sh).
#
# THIS FILE IS PARSED, NEVER SOURCED. Each crss script reads it line by
# line and only accepts lines matching ^CRSS_[A-Z0-9_]+=<value> — anything
# else (comments, blank lines, shell logic, a typo'd/lowercase name) is
# silently ignored. The value is taken LITERALLY: whitespace is significant,
# one optional layer of matching single or double quotes is stripped, and
# nothing is ever eval'd, expanded ($VAR, ~), or command-substituted
# ($(...) / `...` are kept as inert text). So:
#
#   CRSS_WORKSPACE=/srv/code          # -> /srv/code
#   CRSS_WORKSPACE="/srv/code"        # -> /srv/code (quotes stripped)
#   CRSS_WORKSPACE=$HOME/code         # -> the literal string "$HOME/code",
#                                     #    NOT your home directory expanded
#   CRSS_WORKSPACE=~/code             # -> the literal string "~/code"
#
# Use absolute paths, not $HOME or ~. An env var of the same name, already
# set before a script runs, always wins over whatever is written here. This
# file being missing or unreadable is fine — every var below has a built-in
# generic default and the scripts run normally without any overlay at all.
#
# Only variables actually used by the crss scripts are listed below, each
# with its default (the value you get if you leave the line out or comment
# it out).

# CRSS_WORKSPACE — root of repo-backed sessions. new-session's `workspace`
# type (and its `auto` type-inference) looks for $CRSS_WORKSPACE/<foldername>.
# Default: $HOME/workspace
#CRSS_WORKSPACE=/home/youruser/workspace

# CRSS_SESSIONS_DIR — root of utility (non-repo) sessions: monitors,
# managers, one-off tooling. new-session's `sessions` type uses
# $CRSS_SESSIONS_DIR/<foldername>.
# Default: $HOME/.sessions
#CRSS_SESSIONS_DIR=/home/youruser/.sessions

# CRSS_CLAUDE_HOME — where ~/.claude lives: the global skills catalog
# (symlinked into every spawned session), the remote-control first-party
# settings file, and the rules/ dir new-session/session-doctor look for
# crss-host.md under.
# Default: $HOME/.claude
#CRSS_CLAUDE_HOME=/home/youruser/.claude

# CRSS_CLAUDE_BIN — path to the `claude` binary new-session launches inside
# the spawned tmux pane's supervisor loop.
# Default: /usr/bin/claude if it exists and is executable, else whatever
# `command -v claude` resolves to at spawn time (falls back to the bare
# string "claude" if neither is found, so the pane's own PATH decides).
#CRSS_CLAUDE_BIN=/usr/local/bin/claude

# CRSS_SESSION_BACKEND — generic default backend for new sessions. `claude`
# keeps the traditional Claude Code remote-control path; `codex` launches
# the Codex CLI in the same tmux/systemd/supervisor/kickoff flow. You can
# still override per spawn with `new-session --backend claude|codex`.
# Default: claude
#CRSS_SESSION_BACKEND=claude

# CRSS_CODEX_BIN — path to the `codex` binary new-session launches for
# `--backend codex`.
# Default: whatever `command -v codex` resolves to at spawn time (falls back
# to the bare string "codex" if not found, so the pane's PATH decides).
#CRSS_CODEX_BIN=/usr/local/bin/codex

# CRSS_CODEX_ARGS — extra Codex CLI args for `--backend codex`. This is
# intentionally host-owned: put model, sandbox, approval policy, and local
# Codex config overrides here after checking your installed `codex --help`.
# If your selected model triggers a one-time/new-model TUI menu, prefer a
# host-local `-c ...` override here rather than hardcoding that machine's
# preference in crss.
# Default: empty
# Example only:
#CRSS_CODEX_ARGS=-m gpt-5.5 -s read-only -a never

# CRSS_SESSION_PREFIX — the prefix NEW sessions get. tmux uses `<prefix>_`,
# systemd/remote-control uses `<prefix>-` (e.g. prefix "cs" -> tmux `cs_foo-
# 0715-0630`, remote-control `cs-foo-0715-0630`). Every crss script that
# parses an existing session name (session-doctor's tmux_to_base/svc_to_tmux/
# reap-local, session-alias's poisoned-alias guard, session-handoff/
# session-preserve/session-registry's session enumeration) recognises this
# prefix, so generation and parsing always agree. Must match
# ^[a-z][a-z0-9]{0,15}$ (lowercase letters/digits, starts with a letter,
# <=16 chars) — an invalid value is rejected with a warning and the script
# falls back to the generic default below, never to an empty pattern.
# Default: cs
#CRSS_SESSION_PREFIX=cs

# CRSS_LEGACY_PREFIXES — a `|`-separated list of EXTRA prefixes still
# RECOGNISED when PARSING an existing session name but never used to
# generate a new one. Use this when you're changing CRSS_SESSION_PREFIX on a
# host that already has live/parked sessions under the old prefix, so they
# stay visible to session-doctor/session-handoff/etc. instead of going
# invisible to reap and health checks. Each element validates the same way
# as CRSS_SESSION_PREFIX (^[a-z][a-z0-9]{0,15}$); if ANY element is invalid,
# the WHOLE list is dropped (not just the bad element) and a warning is
# printed — the safe direction, since a bad element could otherwise widen
# the alternation into a catch-all pattern in a reap path.
# Default: (empty — no extra prefixes recognised)
#CRSS_LEGACY_PREFIXES=oldhost|oldprefix

# CRSS_PROTECT_NAMES — a `grep -E` alternation of session-name substrings
# that session-doctor.sh's `reap`/`reap-local`/`registry-prune` NEVER touch,
# no matter what. Matched case-insensitively against the tmux/systemd/
# registry name. Generic default is only the skill's own name, so a bare
# install protects its own remote-control bridge session and nothing else.
# If you run other always-on bridge/gateway sessions on this host, list
# their name fragments here too (pipe-separated, no spaces). An explicitly
# EMPTY value here still protects nothing (never turns into "protect
# everything").
# Default: claude-remote
#CRSS_PROTECT_NAMES=claude-remote|my-other-bridge

# CRSS_ALIAS_PROTECT_NAMES — the session-alias.sh equivalent of
# CRSS_PROTECT_NAMES: folders whose name must never be shortened to an
# alias, so the identifying substring survives into the spawned session's
# name (and so CRSS_PROTECT_NAMES above can still match it). Deliberately a
# SEPARATE variable from CRSS_PROTECT_NAMES — it is usually narrower (a
# folder that merely *contains* your skill's own name is normally a regular,
# aliasable dev session). Generic default is empty (no folder is
# alias-protected out of the box).
# Default: (empty)
#CRSS_ALIAS_PROTECT_NAMES=my-other-bridge

# CRSS_JUNK_RE_EXTRA — an ADDITIVE extra alternation appended to the
# built-in "regenerable scaffolding" pattern that session-doctor.sh
# (_WT_IGNORED_DENY_RE) and session-preserve.sh (JUNK_RE) already ship with
# (node_modules, .venv, __pycache__, .gstack, .claude/skills, …). Use it to
# add paths specific to tooling you run on top of crss that isn't part of
# the public skill. Appended as `|<value>` to both patterns — write a
# complete alternation fragment, e.g. `mytool-cache|\.mytoolstate`.
# Default: (empty — no change to the built-in pattern)
#CRSS_JUNK_RE_EXTRA=mytool-cache|\.mytoolstate

# CRSS_HEALTH_SNAPSHOT_DIR — where fleet-status.sh looks for the latest
# server-health-audit JSON snapshot (a directory of UTC-timestamped run
# dirs, each containing summary.json). fleet-status degrades gracefully
# with a "no snapshot found" note if this doesn't exist or nothing has ever
# written to it — it is not a hard dependency.
# Default: empty (the section prints a one-line note and is skipped)
#CRSS_HEALTH_SNAPSHOT_DIR=/home/youruser/.local/state/server-health/runs
